Say which window notification went out, and whether the delegate was listening.

A DIAGNOSTIC, NOT A FIX. It exists because the two ways a delegate can fail to react are
indistinguishable from outside: a notification nobody observes and a notification never posted both
end with the application doing nothing.

WHY IT WAS NEEDED. -setDelegate: wires a delegate to these notifications ONE SELECTOR AT A TIME and
only if the delegate responds AT THAT MOMENT, so a delegate installed before its class is ready, or
swapped later, ends up registered for a different set than it implements. Nothing said so.

WHAT IT SETTLED IMMEDIATELY, in task #247. iTerm2 loses its terminal text on a HEIGHT resize and
keeps it on a width resize, and the suspect was that our compositor path never tells the window it
resized. It does:

    CIDER_WINNOTE window=1 name=NSWindowDidResizeNotification delegate=PseudoTerminal responds=1

five times, on the window the compositor actually resized. So the notification, the delegate and
the selector are all correct and the defect is further in. That is a hypothesis killed in one run.

The mapping from notification to delegate selector covers the ones worth asking the question about.
The rest print responds=0, which means nothing for them and is documented at the call site.

Task #247.
--- a/AppKit/NSWindow.m
+++ b/AppKit/NSWindow.m
@@ -691,6 +691,24 @@
 }
 
 - (void) postNotificationName: (NSNotificationName) name {
+    /*
+     * WHICH WINDOW NOTIFICATION WENT OUT, AND WHETHER ANYONE WAS LISTENING. A delegate is wired to
+     * these one selector at a time in -setDelegate:, and only if it responds at that moment, so a
+     * delegate installed early or swapped later is registered for a DIFFERENT set than it
+     * implements. From outside, a notification nobody observes and a notification never posted look
+     * identical: the application simply does not react. Printing the post AND whether the current
+     * delegate answers the matching selector separates them in one line. Only the notifications
+     * worth asking that about are mapped; the rest print responds=0 and mean nothing. Task #247.
+     */
+    if (getenv("CIDER_TRACE_WINNOTE") != NULL && getenv("CIDER_TRACE_WINNOTE")[0] != (char) 0) {
+        SEL selector = [self _ciderDelegateSelectorForNotification: name];
+
+        fprintf(stderr, "CIDER_WINNOTE window=%ld name=%s delegate=%s responds=%d\n",
+                (long) [self windowNumber], [name UTF8String],
+                _delegate != nil ? object_getClassName(_delegate) : "nil",
+                (selector != NULL && [_delegate respondsToSelector: selector]) ? 1 : 0);
+        fflush(stderr);
+    }
     [[NSNotificationCenter defaultCenter] postNotificationName: name
                                                         object: self];
 }
@@ -1004,6 +1022,26 @@
     }
 }
 
+/* Which delegate method a window notification is delivered as, for the CIDER_TRACE_WINNOTE line.
+ * The pairs are the ones -setDelegate: registers just below. */
+- (SEL) _ciderDelegateSelectorForNotification: (NSNotificationName) name {
+    if ([name isEqualToString: NSWindowDidResizeNotification])
+        return @selector(windowDidResize:);
+    if ([name isEqualToString: NSWindowDidMoveNotification])
+        return @selector(windowDidMove:);
+    if ([name isEqualToString: NSWindowDidEndLiveResizeNotification])
+        return @selector(windowDidEndLiveResize:);
+    if ([name isEqualToString: NSWindowWillStartLiveResizeNotification])
+        return @selector(windowWillStartLiveResize:);
+    if ([name isEqualToString: NSWindowDidBecomeKeyNotification])
+        return @selector(windowDidBecomeKey:);
+    if ([name isEqualToString: NSWindowDidBecomeMainNotification])
+        return @selector(windowDidBecomeMain:);
+    if ([name isEqualToString: NSWindowDidUpdateNotification])
+        return @selector(windowDidUpdate:);
+    return NULL;
+}
